A||N||47||Will||Clearing the air about FWD 3.4||||1||
Z||000000||Will||06-18-2001||08:51 PM||||I've been itching to blow some steam off about this topic for a while...<P>Every time someone asks about FWD 3.4, they invariably receive some version of the following response "The FWD 3.4 is a B**** because you have the change the heads to use the fiero intake and none of the accessory brackets are the same."<P>SO F****** WHAT?!?!<P>The FWD 3.4 makes 20 more HP, 20 more ftlbs, has a wider power band and gets better mileage than the iron head 3.4. So what if your intake doesn't say "Fiero" anymore?<P>Is there a real reason why no one does this swap, or are all the people who utter the above statements lemmings who can't think outside the box?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||88formula||06-18-2001||09:12 PM||||Did you see the counselor lately?  I think your stressed or something. <P><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 06-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fformula88||06-18-2001||09:23 PM||||Wow!  Don't like the iron head 3.4's do we!!<P>I always liked the 3.4 iron head because of the ease oof the upgrade.  Simply get yourself the block and bolt all the Fiero stuff to it.  However, it is an interesting question... how much more work would it be to go to an aluminum head 3.4L?  Of course, if it becomes too much extra effort it may make more sense to step up to a 3.8, or a V8 after that. Reliability would have to be an issue too. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Terrybogin||06-18-2001||09:44 PM||||It is not a big deal if you don't mind changing some wiring. The FWD 3.4 is a DIS motor, a distributer will not fit in it because of the intake manifold design. If you use a '730 ecm with a correct prom (or a reprogrammed prom), it should be fine. If you don't want to fool with changing ECM's and swapping the wires around in the harness, then the iron head 3.4 is your best bet. I guess it depends on whether you are mechanically inclined, drill and tap the starter holes- or electrically inclined, new ECM and wiring changes- either one is a good swap. <BR>For what it's worth- the Fiero wiring harness has pretty much every wire in it already to run a 730 ecm with the DIS brick. It is just a matter of pulling the pins from the Fiero ecm connector and putting them in the new ecm in the correct place.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Will||06-19-2001||12:05 AM||||I know the wiring ain't no thang. It baffles me why people are afraid of a wiring harness.<P>Don't F-body 3.4's start out DIS?<BR>What kind of an upgrade is it to go back to a distributor?<P>If wiring is all the work necessary, then the FWD 3.4 is easier than the 3.8 because the 3.8 requires rewiring <I>and</I> fabrication of mount brackets.<P>The FWD 3.4 can stil use the Fiero engine mount bracket, right?<P>Have the FWD 3.4's ever been offered with a manual trans? IOW, does the 730 ECM expect to see an automatic transmission?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fiero_Freak||06-19-2001||12:14 AM||||I'm going with the iron head simply for the fact that I'm planning on a turbo.  With heat being a concern I just want to play it on the safe side.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero_Left_01.jpg"> <BR>Two '87 GT's...<BR>   ...Double Trouble||||reg||1
Z||000006||Wannabe||06-19-2001||01:19 AM||||I'm abosolutely with you on this, Will. The consensus seems to be "the AL heads are not worth the trouble". Let's get a dialogue going on this. I was planning to go it alone when the tear down starts in the fall.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Standard||06-19-2001||02:00 AM||||Actually, the aluminum heads flow much much much better than the iron heads... it's just alot of trouble to put al. heads on an iron head engine, because you need different pistons, intake, hafta switch to DIS, etc etc..||||reg||1
Z||000008||00lE||06-19-2001||06:12 AM||||There are also some problems with the exhaust system that some would find discouraging.  The GEN III aluminum heads use a D-shaped exhaust port that will not work with the Fiero exhaust manifolds, so a custom exhaust system is needed.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Will||06-19-2001||09:18 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 00lE:<BR><B>There are also some problems with the exhaust system that some would find discouraging.  The GEN III aluminum heads use a D-shaped exhaust port that will not work with the Fiero exhaust manifolds, so a custom exhaust system is needed.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Not a custom exhaust, just custom headers made to meet up with one of the aftermarket Y pipes currently available.<P>For that matter, you could modify some of the aftermarket headers available to work with D ports. Unless the port spacing is different...<p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 06-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Will||06-19-2001||09:23 AM||||It seems to me that the GM 60 degree 6 platform is not living up to its potential. Along the lines of my "internal dimensions of 3.4" post, does anyone know the following dimensions?<P>Rod length of 2.8/3.1/3.4?<BR>Stroke of 2.8?<BR>Stroke of 3.1/3.4?<BR>Bore of 2.8/3.1?<BR>Bore of 3.4?<P>If you put a 2.8 crank into a 3.4 with 3.4 pistons and custom rods, you'd end up with a 3.1 with a higher rod/stroke ratio than a standard 3.1. I'm trying to find out if it would be enough higher to be worth losing 300 cc of displacement.<BR>And if you're going to all this trouble, you might as well use the aluminum heads, roller cam, and advanced electronics.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Black88GT||06-19-2001||09:47 AM||||Most do the 3.4 swap because its a step above a 2.8 and its by far the easiest of them all.  <P>If I am going through all the hassle of wiring and exhaust work, why would I NOT use the 3800, which we can all agree is a better engine then a 60 degree V6.  The price of a few brackets is worth the Hp/torque/reliability gain.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Fformula88||06-19-2001||10:55 AM||||I've got to agree with Black88 on this one.  If your going to go thru the trouble of getting or reprogramming the ECM, and rewiring the car for the DIS engines, it would seem to make more sense to go with the 3800.  For that matter, might as well make it a 3800 SC!||||reg||1
Z||000013||Formula88||06-19-2001||11:11 AM||||My personal thoughts are, if I can do the 3.4 Iron Head and keep it looking all original, great!  More power and it looks original.  Now, if I'm changing to the Al head engine for that extra 20HP (I'm not getting into converting an Fe head engine to an Al head - I'm assuming you're starting with the complete engine) I lose the original look. If that's no problem, then why not go for a 3800SC or 3.4TDC?  20HP to loose the stock look is a waste, IMO.  If I loose the stock look, I want an extra 50 or 100 HP - or more.  Let's see, 180HP 3.4 - doesn't look stock, or a 240HP 3800SC - doesn't look stock.  See my logic?  And you can get a 3800SC up to about 300HP real easy.  Just ask Phil!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I'll probably do the Fe Head 3.4 for my Formula to keep it looking original.  (OEM Believability).  For my planned future kit car, I'll likely use a V8 or 3800SC, since original appearance won't matter.  ||||reg||6
Z||000014||Steve Normington||06-19-2001||11:21 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B>The FWD 3.4 can stil use the Fiero engine mount bracket, right?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I am in the midst of a Al 3.4 swap and I would say that I wouldn't do it it again. I'm using the stock pistons/heads so there are many mods required. The two tranny mounts fit fine, the front engine requires grinding of the oil pan to fit. The driveline absorber is using two of the three bolts to mount to the engine, the dogbone bracket and cross bracket require grinding and welding to fit. The alternator, upper, and lower AC brackets have to be custom welded since the bolt pattern in the side of the Al heads is different from the Fe heads. The EGR tube has to be custom shaped since the 3.4 intake is designed to have the EGR valve on the intake.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Will||06-19-2001||02:00 PM||||Why are you doing all that?<BR>Why can you not just use the stock accessory mounting brackets from the FWD 3.4?<P>Just pull the 3.4 out of the FWD, drop it into your car. Don't mess with the accessories, except to remove the vanes from the P/S pump; don't mess with the intake manifold.<BR>Admittedly, I haven't crawled around one of these things, but why is all that work necessary?<P>The only thing I can see from the picture at <A HREF="http://www.gmpowertrain.com/engines_cartruck/other/34_main.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.gmpowertrain.com/engines_cartruck/other/34_main.htm</A>  is that the alternator might interfere with the fiero dogbone bracket. How much else is there that I can't see formt aht picture?||||reg||1
Z||000016||Steve Normington||06-19-2001||04:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B>How much else is there that I can't see formt aht picture?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That picture is not the same as the engine I am using (1997 Van). Here is a closer picture <A HREF="http://www.gmpowertrain.com/engines_cartruck/other/31_main.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.gmpowertrain.com/engines_cartruck/other/31_main.htm</A>  The alternator won't clear the dogbone or the strut tower. The water pump  wouldn't fit unless I cut a hole out of the firewall. The front mount for the engine won't clear the timing cover/tensioner.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000017||Shaun41178||06-19-2001||04:47 PM||||why do you have to weld the alt and a/c Fiero brackets?  You said the position of the mounting holes in the Fiero heads and al heads are different.  So why can't you just redrill the holes of the Fiero mounting brackets to correspond with the aluminum head holes and postion the bracket in a way where it will still clear the shock tower and have a tight belt at the same time?  I too have not looked at all this up close so I of course don't understand it fully myself but I am curious.  Not that I plan on going this route but just for sheer curiosity I would like to know as many details as possible.  Email me if you would rather do that.  Shaun41178@aol.com<P>------------------<BR>Shaun 85 GT with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds<BR><A HREF="http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178" TARGET=_blank>Preferred Performance</A>||||reg||1
Z||000018||88formula||06-19-2001||04:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B>It seems to me that the GM 60 degree 6 platform is not living up to its potential. Along the lines of my "internal dimensions of 3.4" post, does anyone know the following dimensions?<P>Rod length of 2.8/3.1/3.4?<BR>Stroke of 2.8?<BR>Stroke of 3.1/3.4?<BR>Bore of 2.8/3.1?<BR>Bore of 3.4?<P>If you put a 2.8 crank into a 3.4 with 3.4 pistons and custom rods, you'd end up with a 3.1 with a higher rod/stroke ratio than a standard 3.1. I'm trying to find out if it would be enough higher to be worth losing 300 cc of displacement.<BR>And if you're going to all this trouble, you might as well use the aluminum heads, roller cam, and advanced electronics.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The Rod length of a 2.8/3.1/3.4 is 5.7" or the same as a 350 small block Chevy.<BR>The stroke of 2.8 is 2.99"<BR>The stroke of 3.1/3.4 is 3.310"<BR>The bore of 2.8/3.1 is 3.5"<BR>The bore of 3.4 is 3.620"<P>If you had a 3.620" bore and a 2.99" stroke you would have 3.0-liters and 184.6 cid.<P>If you had a 3.50" bore and a 2.99" stroke you would have 2.8-liters and 172.6 cid.<P>If you had a 3.50" bore and a 3.310" stroke you would have 3.1-liters and 191.8 cid. <P>If you had a 3.620" bore and a 3.310" stroke you would have 3.35-liters and 204.4 cid. <P>If you had a 3.650" bore (3.620 overbored .030") and a 3.310" stroke you would have 3.4-liters and 207.8 cid.  <P><BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000019||Steve Normington||06-19-2001||04:59 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Shaun41178:<BR><B>why do you have to weld the alt and a/c Fiero brackets?  You said the position of the mounting holes in the Fiero heads and al heads are different.  So why can't you just redrill the holes of the Fiero mounting brackets to correspond with the aluminum head holes and postion the bracket in a way where it will still clear the shock tower and have a tight belt at the same time?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The bolts in the Al head are farther apart that the bolts in the Fe head, so the bracket doesn't fit over the holes in the Al head. In other words, the Al bolt holes are outside the bracket.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000020||Shaun41178||06-19-2001||07:22 PM||||ok gotcha now I understand poor poor fiero bracket.  ||||reg||1
Z||000021||00lE||06-20-2001||08:12 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B>  Not a custom exhaust, just custom headers made to meet up with one of the aftermarket Y pipes currently available.<P>For that matter, you could modify some of the aftermarket headers available to work with D ports. Unless the port spacing is different...<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sorry, but the flanges on the end of the 3400 exhaust manifold will not work with ANY of the aftermarket Y pipes. There are also no headers made that would fit the D-shaped ports.  I guess its pretty easy to discount this problem until you are actually faced with it.  ||||reg||1
Z||000022||00lE||06-20-2001||08:35 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Steve Normington:<BR><B> The bolts in the Al head are farther apart that the bolts in the Fe head, so the bracket doesn't fit over the holes in the Al head. In other words, the Al bolt holes are outside the bracket.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Why not just tap some new holes into the head?  You might hit some water jackets, but the waterpump bolts already do that anyway.  The Fiero timing cover and wp will fit, you just need to drill out a few of the timing cover bolt holes since the threads in the block are one size larger, but they do line just up fine.  The dogbone bracket is a bit of a challenge since a good portion of it must be removed to clear the valve cover.<P>I think the only thing that makes a swap to this engine desirable is the price of the motors.  The 3100 has been a popular motor for the w-body and various others since 1994.  They can be easily found with little or no mileage for a fraction of the cost of even a 2.8 rebuild.  The 3400 is a slightly newer motor and it will probably be awhile yet before they are available dirt cheap, but it will happen.||||reg||1
Z||000023||Will||06-20-2001||09:13 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 00lE:<BR><B> Sorry, but the flanges on the end of the 3400 exhaust manifold will not work with ANY of the aftermarket Y pipes. There are also no headers made that would fit the D-shaped ports.  I guess its pretty easy to discount this problem until you are actually faced with it.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You weren't listening. "custom headers made to meet up with one of the aftermarket Y pipes currently available" means create headers designed to match the exhaust ports and meet a pre-existing Y-pipe. I said nothing about stock 3400 exhaust manifolds.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000024||00lE||06-20-2001||09:21 AM||||      <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B> You weren't listening. "custom headers made to meet up with one of the aftermarket Y pipes currently available" means create headers designed to match the exhaust ports and meet a pre-existing Y-pipe. I said nothing about stock 3400 exhaust manifolds.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That would be fine if you could find header flanges that would work with the D-shaped ports, but not even "Headers by ED" had anything that would come close to working.<BR>Having been through this ordeal myself, I think I know what I am talking about...<P>My temporary solution was to use the stock exhaust manifolds and just run from the exit at the rear to meet the existing exhaust system right before it curves to connect to the Fiero muffler.  It did result in a very short exhaust pipe (~2 ft) that should flow well, but since it bypasses the cat converter, I still have to install a custom exhaust system in order for it to be emmissions legal.<P>This should give a good idea of why it is not as simple as it would seem:<P>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/gen_3_head.jpg">    <P>A round pipe large enough to accomodate the ports will not fit between the manifold bolts, not to mention the nice little ridge that would have to be machined flat.  If you try using a smaller tube, you create quite an obstruction, and the port design is the major reason for the increased horsepower obtained using the GEN III heads.<P>But on a lighter note, here is an example of what I mean by dirt cheap :<BR> <A HREF="http://cgi.ebay.com/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=592366805" TARGET=_blank>http://cgi.ebay.com/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=592366805</A> <P><p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 06-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000025||Steve Normington||06-20-2001||10:42 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 00lE:<BR><B> Why not just tap some new holes into the head?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I thought about that, but there is no reinforcing in the head where the bolts would go. I didn't trust mounting everything to 1/4" of cast aluminum.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000026||Will||06-20-2001||02:52 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88formula:<BR><B> The Rod length of a 2.8/3.1/3.4 is 5.7" or the same as a 350 small block Chevy.<BR>The stroke of 2.8 is 2.99"<BR>The stroke of 3.1/3.4 is 3.310"<BR>The bore of 2.8/3.1 is 3.5"<BR>The bore of 3.4 is 3.620"<P>If you had a 3.620" bore and a 2.99" stroke you would have 3.0-liters and 184.6 cid.<P>If you had a 3.50" bore and a 2.99" stroke you would have 2.8-liters and 172.6 cid.<P>If you had a 3.50" bore and a 3.310" stroke you would have 3.1-liters and 191.8 cid. <P>If you had a 3.620" bore and a 3.310" stroke you would have 3.35-liters and 204.4 cid. <P>If you had a 3.650" bore (3.620 overbored .030") and a 3.310" stroke you would have 3.4-liters and 207.8 cid.  <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Thank you. Finally, someone answers the question!<P>So, a stock 2.8 has a rod/stroke ratio of 1.906. That's quite good.<BR>A 3.1/3.4 has an R/S ratio of 1.722. Not as good, but still better than most Honda engines.<BR>With 3.4 pistons and a 2.8 crank, an engine would need 5.86" rods and a rod/stroke ratio of 1.959<BR>Using 5.900" rods from an L99 (4.3L version of LT1 used in Caprices), with custom pistons, the engine would have an R/S ratio of 1.973.<P>Custom pistons may not be required with a thick head gasket and <I>very</I> carefully set quench height.<P>Anyway, 1.973 is significantly better than 1.722. I'll have to build one to see if it makes up for the displacement loss from 3.4. I'm sure it would perform better than a 3.1.<P>88Formula, you have desktop dyno, or something like that, don't you?<BR>Could you run this setup through it and see what it says:<BR>2.99 stroke, 3.620 bore, 5.900 rod<BR>everything else same as stock 3400 SFI<P>Is it advanced enough to take R/S ratio into account?<P>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000027||Will||06-20-2001||04:15 PM||||Steve, 00|E, thanks for your input on this subject.<P>I still have a question: Did the pan rails, crank seals, or something else change which prevents the Fiero pan from being used on the FWD 3.4 block?||128.173.105.164||reg||1
Z||000028||Wade87GT||06-20-2001||05:16 PM||||O.K., I used to be a techy, but I lost track. So, I need to ask this again since I didn't get an answer the first time. Can you easily use the late model FWD 3.4 shortblock (Aztec) with the Fiero heads, intake, exhaust, etc? Since the Aztec is rated at 185 HP (in one piece) this shortblock should be a much better starting point than the low compression combo you get from the early minivan/Lumina shortblock and Fiero intake, heads, etc. Or what?||||reg||5
Z||000029||Will||06-20-2001||05:31 PM||||Someone on the forum (don't remember who right now) used a roller cam block with F-body 3.4 pistons and Fiero iron heads and top end. So yes, that should work.||||reg||1
Z||000030||Steve Normington||06-20-2001||05:41 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B>I still have a question: Did the pan rails, crank seals, or something else change which prevents the Fiero pan from being used on the FWD 3.4 block?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The pan bolt pattern on the 3.4 is wider across the crank than the Fiero block. Also, the 3.4 bearing caps bolt to the oil pan for added rigidity.||||reg||1
Z||000031||88formula||06-20-2001||05:42 PM||||Will,<P>Dyno2000 doesn't take into account rod length ratio directly. You can't type in the rod length and get the rod ratio. When you are concerned with stroke to rod ratios I assume you are worried about frictional losses, piston longevity, and piston dwell time. The program does take into account frictional losses but I don't know how.  <P>The power for a completely stock 3.4-liter engine (3.620 bore & 3.310 stroke) with the stock restrictive exhaust manifolds and mufflers looks like this.<P>RPM....POWER...TORQUE<BR>2000...72...188<BR>2500...91...190<BR>3000...114..199<BR>3500...132..199<BR>4000...146..192<BR>4500...154..179<BR>5000...155..163<BR>5500...151..144<BR>6000...139..121<BR>6500...119..96<P>The same engine but now with a 2.99" stroke looks like this. <P>2000...63...166<BR>2500...81...169<BR>3000...103..180<BR>3500...122..183<BR>4000...137..179<BR>4500...146..171<BR>5000...151..159<BR>5500...151..144<BR>6000...143..125<BR>6500...129..104<P><BR>If the rod is shorter ,relatively the engine compression ratio can be increased, since the piston will stay at the top of the block for a shorter period with a shorter rod There is less time for pre-ignition and detonation to occur. Detonation will destroy the strongest engine. With a high compression ratio the engine gets better gas mileage and makes more power. It’s more efficient in terms of pumping air.<P> A longer rod will give the piston more leverage to turn the crankshaft and dwell at TDC for a longer period of time. It’s easier on the piston and crank and the engine will rev higher and make more power.    <P>Is there an ideal Rod length ratio or it just another compromise?<P>For a street engine I would be more concerned about how much air the engine can pump (displacement), pumping losses (exhaust, headers, heads, manifold type) and if I was doing a rebuild things like quench area (piston to head clearance) and the quality of the machine work and parts. <P>The pumping loses alone account for about 40% of the frictional losses in power. That’s why exhaust systems, cylinder heads, the headers make such a huge difference.  <P><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 06-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000032||88formula||06-20-2001||05:50 PM||||I forgot to mention that many underestimate the horsepower potential of the cast iron heads. The cast iron heads are capable of supporting 300 naturally aspired horsepower with little head porting. There is a forum member here that has a naturally aspired 60-degree v6 engine that makes 270-horsepower and the engine has an 8000-rpm reline. He doesn't have the fiero's fuel injection of course. He has a 390 CFM four barrel carburetor. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000033||Will||06-20-2001||05:56 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B>Someone on the forum (don't remember who right now) used a roller cam block with F-body 3.4 pistons and Fiero iron heads and top end. So yes, that should work.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The member is fierosound. The engine page is here: <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/calgaryfieros/details.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/calgaryfieros/details.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000034||Will||06-20-2001||06:25 PM||||A longer rod means at least three things:<P>1) more piston dwell time and increased octane tolerance as 88formula mentioned. This allows the engine to run more boost or higher compression on the same octane. <P>2) less frictional loss and longer engine life because of less side loading of the pistons. Also higher RPM capacity because the cylinder walls are not loaded as much, reducing the likelihood that a con-rod will go through the block at high RPM.<P>3) the one that most people overlook: more efficient transfer of force from piston to crankshaft. Because the rod angle gets smaller as the rod length goes up, and the force applied to the crank pin is a function of the cosine of the rod angle, the fraction of the force on the piston which is transferred to the crankpin will be higher as rod length goes up. This is linked to the lower side loading. The force that goes into side loading with a short con-rod goes into the crank pin with a long con-rod.<P>88formula: could you try a couple of simulations with compression ratios of 10.5:1, 11:1, 11.5:1?<P>I've heard that 1.75 is the ideal R/S ratio for an "acceleration" engine. The 327 SBC has a 1.75 R/S ratio, as does the B16 Honda, which goes to 10K RPM often enough to be noticed.<BR>Everything I have read, however, makes me think "the higher the better" for R/S ratio. The 377 SBC, PHR's 4.155 bore x 3.25 stroke 350 with 1.91 R/S ratio (11:1 compression on 87 octane!), and the L99 V8 with 9.95 compression, iron heads, 1.95+ R/S ratio which never skips a beat on 87 octane all work together to convince me that the higher the R/S ratio the better.<P>I'd like to build a Can-Am 430 BBC (4.440 bore, 3.47 stroke) in a high deck Merlin block with a R/S ratio of 2.3+||||reg||1
Z||000035||88formula||06-21-2001||06:05 PM||||RPM horsepower torque with 10-1 compression 3.4-liter <P>2000 75.047325 196.999222<BR>2500 94.829277 199.141479<BR>3000 118.996765 208.244339<BR>3500 138.544113 207.816162<BR>4000 153.111801 200.959244<BR>4500 161.845840 188.820145<BR>5000 164.477570 172.701447<BR>5500 160.559555 153.261398<BR>6000 149.585785 130.887558<BR>6500 130.800217 105.646332<P>RPM horsepower torque with 10.5-1 compression 3.4-liter <P>2000 76.533463 200.900345<BR>2500 96.711418 203.093979<BR>3000 121.315529 212.302170<BR>3500 141.235367 211.853043<BR>4000 156.271027 205.105728<BR>4500 165.457611 193.033875<BR>5000 168.490662 176.915192<BR>5500 164.981232 157.482086<BR>6000 154.368637 135.072556<BR>6500 136.005417 109.850525<P>RPM horsepower torque with 11-1 compression 3.4-liter <P>2000 77.934586 204.578293<BR>2500 98.507507 206.865753<BR>3000 123.464729 216.063278<BR>3500 143.791855 215.687775<BR>4000 159.136902 208.867188<BR>4500 168.784821 196.915619<BR>5000 172.255630 180.868408<BR>5500 169.073700 161.388535<BR>6000 158.740173 138.897644<BR>6500 140.954254 113.847672<P>RPM horsepower torque with 10-1 compression 3.0-liter (3.620 bore and 2.99 stroke)<P>2000 66.097977 173.507187<BR>2500 84.461006 177.368118<BR>3000 107.538422 188.192230<BR>3500 127.376045 191.064072<BR>4000 142.941879 187.611221<BR>4500 153.606216 179.207245<BR>5000 159.169464 167.127945<BR>5500 159.462540 152.214233<BR>6000 153.282516 134.122192<BR>6500 139.607330 112.759766<P>RPM horsepower torque with 10.5-1 compression 3.0-liter (3.620 bore and 2.99 stroke)<P><BR>2000 67.426865 176.995514<BR>2500 86.165680 180.947922<BR>3000 109.608238 191.814423<BR>3500 129.805695 194.708542<BR>4000 145.778503 191.334290<BR>4500 156.832077 182.970749<BR>5000 162.791641 170.931229<BR>5500 163.520844 156.088074<BR>6000 157.558395 137.863586<BR>6500 144.453323 116.673836<P>RPM horsepower torque with 11-1 compression 3.0-liter (3.620 bore and 2.99 stroke)<P>2000 68.694328 180.322617<BR>2500 87.783875 184.346130<BR>3000 111.529320 195.176315<BR>3500 132.063660 198.095490<BR>4000 148.398163 194.772583<BR>4500 159.802444 186.436188<BR>5000 166.124054 174.430267<BR>5500 167.274002 159.670639<BR>6000 161.645264 141.439606<BR>6500 148.828369 120.207535<P>Here is my 3.4-liter engine (3.650 bore and 3.310 stroke) with ported heads, GM 12363220 camshaft (same as Crane PowerMax 260), Tuned headers (I hope), and 9.06 compression. <P>RPM horsepower torque<P>2000 85.146133 223.508591<BR>2500 108.985390 228.869308<BR>3000 132.865631 232.514862<BR>3500 157.988708 236.983063<BR>4000 180.504807 236.912567<BR>4500 196.137299 228.826843<BR>5000 203.215149 213.375900<BR>5500 201.650436 192.484512<BR>6000 188.897110 165.284973<BR>6500 169.651627 137.026321<P>Here is an example of what these engines could do in an 88 formula with a 2950 lb. weight including driver and the five speed Getrag transmission. <P>3.0-liter with 11-1 compression and everything else completely stock, including the restrictive exhaust. <BR>0-60 mph in 6.642<BR>Quarter mile in 14.815 seconds @ 91.2 mph<P>3.4-liter with 11-1 compression and everything else completely stock, including the restrictive exhaust.<BR>0-60 mph in 6.398 seconds <BR>Quarter mile in 14.603 seconds @ 92.2 mph.  <P>Will, wouldn’t a longer rod give you LESS octane tolerance not more because of the longer piston dwell time? That is what I was trying to say in the first post. I was tired when I wrote that one. <P>That was a lot of work! <P><BR> <P><BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000036||Will||06-21-2001||07:44 PM||||The longer piston dwell time prevents detonation. I'm not really sure how to explain the effect, or even if I fully understand it, but it's well documented. I thin it has to do with the rate of change of pressure within the combustion chamber as the flame front is propagating. (rate of change of pressure is lower with longer rod)<P>Thanks for doing all that work.<p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 06-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000037||BV MotorSports||06-22-2001||12:48 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR>[B]It seems to me that the GM 60 degree 6 platform is not living up to its potential. Along the lines of my "internal dimensions of 3.4" post, does anyone know the following dimensions?<P>Rod length of 2.8/3.1/3.4?<BR>Stroke of 2.8?<BR>Stroke of 3.1/3.4?<BR>Bore of 2.8/3.1?<BR>Bore of 3.4?<P>B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Try here:<BR> <A HREF="http://members.aol.com/_ht_a/gpxss/index2.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.aol.com/_ht_a/gpxss/index2.html</A> <BR>GM 60deg V6 page<P>Steven<P><P>------------------<BR>87 Fiero GT 14.9/ 93mph<BR>94 Formula WS6 w/ NOS 12.4 /118mph<BR>87 Conquest TSi <BR>85.5 Starion ESi||||reg||1
Z||000038||87GTZ34||06-22-2001||11:22 AM||||Will - that is my understanding as well. It has more impact on heads/pistons with big quench areas which evens out the pressurization rate of a small volume.  Also, with narrower angle approach you can play with ignition timing as well to maximize the effect max pressure timing since it changes the compression rate.  All out racing engines try to achieve a 3/1 rod ratio but production blocks & cranks won't allow that. Also additional loading on the crank (at those rates) will shorten engine life but for racing it doesn't matter.  I was looking at your reconfiguration w/ regard to using the 3.4 pistons and 2.8 crank.  Why wouldn't you want to use the 3.4 pistons (higher pin height) with the 2.8 crank and early SBC (2" crankpin) 6" long rods.  The rods need to be milled slightly on one side (narrowed) for the 60 degree crank. This would give 2/1 rod ratio and you would have .020 to get get rid of to get back to target compression (gasket & head or deck milling).<BR>3.31 - 2.99 = .32; 6" - 5.7" = .3 with .020 left over.<P>There are also 6.25 rods but custom slugs would need to be cut.  I considered doing this at one time with a 3.4 pushrod but then I got the DOHC.  I may still do it as I need to drop some $$ on custom slugs but need to check it out further with regard to valve and piston timing.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000039||Will||06-22-2001||02:32 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 87GTZ34:<BR><B>Will - that is my understanding as well. It has more impact on heads/pistons with big quench areas which evens out the pressurization rate of a small volume.  Also, with narrower angle approach you can play with ignition timing as well to maximize the effect max pressure timing since it changes the compression rate.  All out racing engines try to achieve a 3/1 rod ratio but production blocks & cranks won't allow that. Also additional loading on the crank (at those rates) will shorten engine life but for racing it doesn't matter.  I was looking at your reconfiguration w/ regard to using the 3.4 pistons and 2.8 crank.  Why wouldn't you want to use the 3.4 pistons (higher pin height) with the 2.8 crank and early SBC (2" crankpin) 6" long rods.  The rods need to be milled slightly on one side (narrowed) for the 60 degree crank. This would give 2/1 rod ratio and you would have .020 to get get rid of to get back to target compression (gasket & head or deck milling).<BR>3.31 - 2.99 = .32; 6" - 5.7" = .3 with .020 left over.<P>There are also 6.25 rods but custom slugs would need to be cut.  I considered doing this at one time with a 3.4 pushrod but then I got the DOHC.  I may still do it as I need to drop some $$ on custom slugs but need to check it out further with regard to valve and piston timing.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You're math is off. You need to divide by two because you're going from diameter to radius:<P>3.31 - 2.99 = .32 (diameter); 0.32/2 = 0.16 (radius)<BR>6" - 5.7" = .3 with 0.140 deficit.<P>However: 5.900" - 5.700" = 0.200 - 0.160 = 0.040" deficit, which might be made up for with a thicker than stock head gasket and tighter than stock quench. How tight was the stock quench?<p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 06-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000040||Formula88||06-22-2001||03:07 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 00lE:<BR><B>      That would be fine if you could find header flanges that would work with the D-shaped ports, but not even "Headers by ED" had anything that would come close to working.<BR>Having been through this ordeal myself, I think I know what I am talking about...<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, there are headers for the 3400, or they are in development.  A company called "The Other Guys" was making them earlier this year for about $900 a set.  These are to fit the 3400 in the 99+ Grand Ams, so they may not be useful in the Fiero, but I thought I'd throw that out.  They have a website, but I've been unable to find the URL.  If I run across it, I'll post it.||||reg||4
Z||000041||87GTZ34||06-22-2001||04:58 PM||||Uh huh.  Didn't pull out my spread sheet from the calculations of 2 yrs ago.  But now that you mention it, I was looking for clearance not shedding it. I mentioned the enhanced effect on quench setups because I assumed the engines you mentioned use quench areas with those high compression ratios.  The 60 degree iron heads have a pseudo quench area with the flat head but piston is also flat not raised.  I haven't measured it, but I would guess the stock quench is somewhere in the neighborhood of .040 for emissions/reliability reasons.  For my application (which isn't complete BTW), I was looking at custom pistons (no forged available for the dohc) focusing on the crown thickness to cheat on the rods.  The dohc uses a flat piston with the 4 valve reliefs and a domed chamber.  Also, I haven't seen 5.9" rods with the 2" crankpin (earlier sbc) so I would have to modify 6" rods or use the rods and modify the pistons.  I think the .040 off the top of forged pistons is possible, but not sure about the .140.  I need to expand my micrometer set to finish measuring the stock pistons.<P>Sounds like that 430 would have some serious torque!  What tranny will you bolt it up to?||||reg||1
Z||000042||Steve Normington||06-22-2001||05:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B>These are to fit the 3400 in the 99+ Grand Ams, so they may not be useful in the Fiero, but I thought I'd throw that out.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Is the 3400 the same engine as a 3.4?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000043||88formula||06-22-2001||05:15 PM||||Mine varied from .060” to more than .075”. The deck was not parallel to the main and it was not a true 60-degree angle. I fixed that when I decked the block.  The crankshaft stroke varied from crankpin to crankpin slightly, which also through things off.<P>You got to love production GM engines.  <P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000044||87GTZ34||06-22-2001||06:33 PM||||88 Did you measure quench afer you had your block and heads machined?  .075 justifies the catalytic converter...  Also did you get quotes on blueprinting the crank?  I may have mine done depending on cost but I haven't checked around yet.<BR>TIA||||reg||1
Z||000045||88formula||06-22-2001||09:34 PM||||Originally I had a .044 quench but now I have .050" quench and the deck is square and perallel to the main. The first machinist I had did a good job on the deck but he screwed up the rods so I had to have them reconditioned again somewhere else. When the other place reconditioned the rods they shrank in height a little, which increased the quench height. As long as I am under .060” I should be OK. I really wanted .040” though. Blueprinting a crankshaft doesn’t cost much more and it’s worth it. <P>The first machinist was cheap and I got what I paid for. He took forever to get the job done to and I had to hound the guy to get it down. If I were you I would choose a machinist based on reputation and quality of work and not cost like I originally did.<P>You really do get what you pay for!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000046||Will||06-24-2001||08:50 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 87GTZ34:<BR><B>Also, I haven't seen 5.9" rods with the 2" crankpin (earlier sbc) so I would have to modify 6" rods or use the rods and modify the pistons.  I think the .040 off the top of forged pistons is possible, but not sure about the .140.  I need to expand my micrometer set to finish measuring the stock pistons.<P>Sounds like that 430 would have some serious torque!  What tranny will you bolt it up to?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ooops. My bad. The L99 version of the LT1 uses 5.940 rods on 2.100 journals, so using those rods would mean one would have to figure out how to lose 0.080, not just 0.040.<BR>I'm not sure if they make spacer bearings that thick or not. Maybe custom offset spacer bearings?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> You might get back 0.050 that way.<P>I'm not sure how familiar you are with the 430, but it was used in the Canadian American Challenge cup in the late 60's and early 70's by <I>McLaren</I> of all teams. It uses a 4.440 bore and 3.470 stroke. Even with normal deck height it kicks @$$, but it would be an unbelievable beast in a high deck block.<BR>Heck, I don't even know what <I>car</I> I'd put the thing in, much less what trans I'd use; I just want to build one. [Insert Tim Taylor Laugh here]<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000047||Wannabe||06-27-2001||06:04 PM||||One more note regarding headers for the d-port heads <A HREF="http://discussions.gmforums.com/showthread.php?s=&threadid=4972" TARGET=_blank>http://discussions.gmforums.com/showthread.php?s=&threadid=4972</A> <P>Lots of neat v660 info on this forum<p>[This message has been edited by Wannabe (edited 06-27-2001).]||||reg||1
